92186097920 #edb/ref 1064 "How can we calculate  $\\displaystyle{\\int} \\left(3\\sin^2 x + 3\\cos^2 x\\right ) \\, \\textrm{d}x?$\n\nWe can compute integrals like this using the Pythagorean trigonometric identity, \n\n$$\n\\sin^2 x + \\cos^2 x=1.\n$$\n\nSo, we evaluate our integral as follows: \n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\int \\left(3\\sin^2 x+  3\\cos^2 x\\right ) \\, \\textrm{d}x  &=  \\int 3(\\underbrace{\\sin^2 x +\\cos^2 x }_1 )\\, \\textrm{d}x\\\n\t\t\t\t                                                                &=\\int 3(1) \\, \\textrm{d}x\\\n\t\t\t\t                                                                &=\\int 3  \\, \\textrm{d}x\\\n\t\t\t\t                                                                &=3x+C\n}\n$$\n\nLet's look at some more examples." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097920 #edb/ref 1065 872 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097920 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "3171a2df-43af-5bd4-9c34-07f328040063" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097920 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097921 #edb/ref 1064 "The identities \n\n$$\n1+\\tan ^{2}x =\\sec ^{2}x\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n1+\\cot ^{2}x =\\csc ^{2}x\n$$\n\nare also called Pythagorean trigonometric identities. They can be used to solve certain integrals involving the expressions \n$\\tan^2 x$ or  $\\csc^2 x.$ Let's see their application in action!" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097921 #edb/ref 1065 874 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097921 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "9ef6112e-50d0-5806-b79e-1e7dfc7ff29f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097921 #edb/ref 1067 "Calculating Integrals Using Related Identities" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247662 #edb/ref 1080 "4(\\pi -2)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247662 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247662 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1a05c2fc-f94d-5489-955e-6e93003cb216" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186248887 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{\\pi }{2}-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186248887 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186248887 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a3dc3121-d85d-555d-95fa-e1f130a553c4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186248911 #edb/ref 1080 "1-\\frac{\\pi }{4}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186248911 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186248911 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a75bb911-b1a2-59de-ac5b-2d5b63e7d0b9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186249176 #edb/ref 1080 "\\pi -3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186249176 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186249176 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c53da061-88b8-5d38-9620-69fbe5f388d5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186249423 #edb/ref 1080 "4-\\pi " #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186249423 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186249423 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e1bfdf52-8608-5e41-81ac-9c0e93199b37" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186249970 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186247662 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186249970 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186248887 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186249970 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186248911 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186249970 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186249176 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186249970 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186249423 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186249970 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186249970 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186249970 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186248911 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186249970 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "a980c72c-15a0-5296-ad73-7e85baf7f354" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250269 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249970 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250269 #edb/ref 1069 "q-114441" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250269 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250269 #edb/ref 1071 "${\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}{cot}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250269 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3841fdbd-8341-5e24-9f59-0d6b99ab3106" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250269 #edb/ref 1074 "In this case, we have to use the identity  $1+{cot}^{2}⁡x={csc}^{2}⁡x.$ Rearranging this gives\n\n$$\n{cot}^{2}⁡x={csc}^{2}⁡x-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}{cot}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}({csc}^{2}⁡x-1)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x-x){|}_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =[-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\frac{\\pi }{2}]-[-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-\\frac{\\pi }{4}] \\\\  & =[-0-\\frac{\\pi }{2}]-[-1-\\frac{\\pi }{4}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+1+\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319780 #edb/ref 1080 "1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319780 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319780 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8074fac7-a6e0-56cc-9165-137d0dae6a7c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186320243 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186319780 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186320243 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186320243 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186320243 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186319780 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186320243 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "22e00bd0-c773-50bc-8df3-96880e42e321" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321026 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320243 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321026 #edb/ref 1069 "q-352697" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321026 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321026 #edb/ref 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}(1+{cot}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321026 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "05d804be-1aa4-52f7-ac45-dafba61557ef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321026 #edb/ref 1074 "We use the cosecant-cotangent identity:\n\n$$\n1+{cot}^{2}⁡x={csc}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we compute the given integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}(1+{cot}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}{csc}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\,{|}_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-(-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})) \\\\  & =-0-(-1) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186428028 #edb/ref 1030 0.3333333333333333 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428028 #edb/ref 1032 "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account foundations: 1 rises / 3 eligible passed source activities = 0.333333; nearest selected fraction = 1/3. Crossing activity numbers [1]; waits [1]. Separate account estimates: foundations=1/3 (1/3). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: True. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/foundations-frequency-estimates.json, pair 1037->1454. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428028 #edb/ref 1036 #edb/ref 17592186049338 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428029 #edb/ref 1030 0.5 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428029 #edb/ref 1032 "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account foundations: 1 rises / 2 eligible passed source activities = 0.500000; nearest selected fraction = 1/2. Crossing activity numbers [2]; waits [2]. Separate account estimates: foundations=1/2 (1/2). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening tar